OS-9/68K FREEWARE COLLECTION  --  start here===========================================480 programs, 356 of which need nothing but this disk, with their source,their documentation, and a record of where each one came from -- threedecades of OS-9 community software, gathered in one place and made to runagain.  The other 124 are starred in DOC/INDEX and want Microware's cio;DOC/README-CIO explains how to supply your own.NOT SURE WHAT YOU ARE LOOKING FOR?  DOC/CATEGORIES groups everything onthe disk by what it is for -- shells, editors, games, screen toys,archivers -- which is the order you want before you know any of thenames.  It covers all 612 programs, counting the 169 netpbm convertersand the gcc passes that the 425 above leaves out.  DOC/INDEX remains thealphabetical account, and says more about each one.OS-9 itself is Microware's, and still a current product.  This is the softwarethe community wrote for it.------------------------------------------------------------------STARTING IT------------------------------------------------------------------TWO WAYS.  The first needs nothing but this disk.1.  STANDALONE -- no Microware modules at all        OS9DISK=/absolute/path/to/this/disk  os9exec /dd/CMDS/bash /dd/SYS/login    SYS/login sets PATH, HOME, TERM and TERMCAP and hands you an    interactive shell.  Started bare instead, bash has none of them, so    no command is found and the full-screen programs look locked up when    they are only missing TERM.  Setting HOME is also what makes bash    read /dd/.bashrc, where cd and pwd are defined -- bash's own pwd    hangs the shell here.  See DOC/README-RUNNING.    bash is a public-domain build and is this disk's shell.  Verified    running with this disk as the ONLY disk and no shell, cio, csl or math    module present anywhere.    RUN COMMANDS BY THEIR FULL PATH:        /dd/CMDS/cookie        /dd/CMDS/soundex </dd/SYS/motd        /dd/CMDS/GAMES/advent            (after: cd /dd/GAMES/ADV)    bash's PATH search does not work against this filesystem -- setting PATH    and typing a bare `cookie' gives "command not found" even though the    program is there and runs perfectly when named in full.  Absolute paths    always work.  This is a wart, not a broken disk.    Do NOT write OS9DISK=./disk with a leading "./" -- modules still load but    every ordinary file open fails.  Use an absolute path.2.  WITH YOUR OWN OS-9 SYSTEM -- the fuller experience        OS9DISK=/path/to/this/disk  OS9H1=/path/to/your/os9/system        os9exec -r /h1/CMDS/shell    This disk is /dd, so every program finds its own data; Microware's shell,    its utilities, and cio/csl/math come from /h1.  Bare command names work,    and so do the 124 starred programs which need cio.    Everything on this disk was tested this way.------------------------------------------------------------------AS A SINGLE DISK IMAGE------------------------------------------------------------------    This collection also ships as one RBF disk image -- 182 MB, of which    about 138 MB is free for score files, saves and your own work.        OS9DISK=/path/to/dd  os9exec /dd/CMDS/bash    The image carries proper OS-9 owner and permission bits, which a host    directory cannot: every module is e+pe+r+pr, every data file r+pr with    no execute.  On a host directory those bits are whatever your Mac or    Linux box happened to have, which is meaningless here.    The image file must be named `dd' (or h0..hz as a secondary device):    os9exec resolves a disk image by FILENAME, so `freeware.rbf' will not    mount.  That is an os9exec limitation, not a property of the image.------------------------------------------------------------------WHERE TO LOOK------------------------------------------------------------------    DOC/STATUS        what runs, what needs cio, what does not run.  START HERE    DOC/INDEX         one line saying what every program IS    DOC/ORIGINS       one line saying where every program CAME FROM    DOC/README-DOCS   the five ways to find out how to use something    DOC/README-RUNNING  deployment, mtools, DOS disks, GNU chess    DOC/EFFO-INFO     the authors' own descriptions AND licence conditions    DOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT   math.l is single precision; math881.l is not    SOURCES.txt       provenance, build method, and what was left out and why    DOC/<program>/    per-program documentation, 114 directories    SRC/<package>/    the source everything was built from, 131 trees------------------------------------------------------------------WHAT IS ON IT------------------------------------------------------------------    CMDS/          418 commands        CMDS/GAMES/   62 programs    CMDS/BROKEN/     2 that do not run, kept because they deserve attention    CMDS/REBUILT/   10 alternate builds of names already in CMDS    CMDS/GCC139/, CMDS/GCC2/          GNU C 1.39 and 2.x    SBPROLOG/      SB-Prolog 2.2 with its whole module library    SRC/, DOC/     source and documentation for essentially all of it    A toolchain (yacc, flex, make, patch, RCS, sdb, indent), five languages    (SB-Prolog, XLISP, Forth, FORTRAN, m4), four editors, pdraw plotting, an    ispell with its dictionary, mtools for DOS disks, nroff with its macros,    a ray tracer, and 57 games.------------------------------------------------------------------LICENCE------------------------------------------------------------------    Public domain and freeware throughout.  FOUR programs carry a condition    their authors asked for -- "no military use" -- and one of those is by    os9exec's own author.  They are named in DOC/EFFO-INFO and marked in    DOC/INDEX.  Please pass the condition on with the software.    CMDS/NONFREE holds six Microware binaries.  Those are not ours to give    away.  Everything else is.